# Values and errors

A native receives HQL values and returns one. This page covers the Rust
type that holds them, arguments written by name, records of a type the
package declares, and the two ways a call fails.

## `Value`

Every HQL value is a `hql::ext::Value`, an enum with one variant per kind
of value. The ones a package meets most:

| HQL type | Variant | Holds |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `Int` | `Value::Int(i64)` | a 64-bit integer |
| `Float` | `Value::Float(f64)` | a double |
| `Bool` | `Value::Bool(bool)` | |
| `String` | `Value::Str(Arc<str>)` | text |
| `List<T>` | `Value::List(Arc<Vec<Value>>, TypeRef)` | the elements and the element type |
| a record type | `Value::Record(Arc<Record>)` | the type and the fields in declaration order |
| `Option<T>`, present | `Value::Present(Arc<Value>)` | the value |
| `Option<T>`, absent | `Value::Absent(TypeRef)` | the type of what is absent |
| a function | `Value::Function(…)` | applied through the context, never by hand |

A list is built with `Value::list(elements, element_type)`, and the common
types are constants: `TypeRef::INT`, `TypeRef::FLOAT`, `TypeRef::STR`.

A native matches on the variant the contract promises. `shout` is declared
on a `String` and on a `Greeting`, so its one function reads both:

```rust
fn shout(
    _cx: &mut dyn EvalCx,
    input: Value,
    _arguments: &[TypedArg],
    span: Span,
) -> Result<Value, Diagnostic> {
    let said = match &input {
        Value::Record(record) => match record.field("text") {
            Some(held) => text(held, &span)?,
            None => return Err(ext::failure(span, "expected a `Greeting`")),
        },
        other => text(other, &span)?,
    };
    Ok(Value::Str(said.to_uppercase().into()))
}
```

## Arguments

`input` is evaluated when the function is called. The other arguments are
not: each is a `TypedArg`, the argument's expression with the name it was
written with. A function finds the ones it wants and evaluates them:

| Function | Gives |
| --- | --- |
| `ext::named(arguments, "punctuation")` | the argument written `punctuation = …`, if the call wrote it |
| `ext::positional(arguments, 0)` | the first argument written without a name, after the input |
| `ext::value_of(cx, argument)` | the argument's value |

An argument with a default that the call leaves out is not in
`arguments`. The function supplies the default the contract states:

```rust
let punctuation = match ext::named(arguments, "punctuation") {
    Some(argument) => text(&ext::value_of(cx, argument)?, &span)?,
    None => "!".to_owned(),
};
```

<!-- run: --workspace examples/packages -->
```hql
import hello

g = Greeting("Ada", punctuation = "?")
[g.text, str(g.length)]
```

```text
["Hello, Ada?", "11"]
```

## Records

A value of a record type the contract declares is built with
`ext::record`: the type by its name, then the fields in the order the
contract declares them.

```rust
Ok(ext::record(
    ext::ty("Greeting", []),
    [
        ("text", Value::Str(said.into())),
        ("length", Value::Int(length)),
    ],
))
```

`ext::ty("Greeting", [])` is the type `Greeting` with no type arguments.
The name is the one the contract declares; the contract is where the
fields and their types are stated, and the Rust follows it. An optional
field, `target? : List<Float>`, holds a `Value::Present` or a
`Value::Absent`.

## Failures

A function that cannot give a result returns a diagnostic.
`ext::failure(span, message)` makes one that points at the call:

```rust
let length = i64::try_from(said.chars().count())
    .map_err(|_| ext::failure(span.clone(), "the greeting is too long"))?;
```

The message is one sentence for the person who wrote the program. It says
what was wrong with the value, in the program's terms: `row 1 has 2
features; row 0 has 1`, not the name of a Rust error.

A failure is an ordinary failure of the program. Under `collect`, or
handled by the steps of [[12-errors|chapter 12]], it behaves as a failure
of a standard function does.

## Panics

A Rust panic inside a native does not end `hql`. `hql::native!` runs each
function under a guard that catches a panic and returns it as a failure
of the call, naming the function:

<!-- run: skip -->
```hql
import hello

hello.fail("boom")
```

```text
execute failure[invalid-argument]: the extension function `fail` panicked: boom
 --> <expression>:3:1
  |
3 | hello.fail("boom")
  | ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
```

The guard is a net, and a failure is the intended path. A panic reports a
defect of the package: an index out of range, an `unwrap` on nothing. A
value a program can pass is refused with `ext::failure`.
